course: dictionary
lesson: 6920
title: Gentle words, hard meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound soft and kind but hide something harsh. Pacification is one of them. In everyday life, to pacify someone is to calm them down, like a parent who pacifies a crying baby with a cuddle. The word means making people calm, or stopping them being angry.
explain: History texts use the word differently. «Pacification» means violently crushing the people of a place until they stop resisting. So the word can describe an army using force to take control of an area, even though it sounds peaceful. Ask yourself who is doing the calming, and how.
explain: Look for clues. Words such as army, force, violence or fighting near the word point to the harsh meaning, not the gentle one. Then swap in your meaning and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Pacification is a noun, and it is uncountable: you say "the pacification", not "a pacification".
fact: Pacify is the verb: to pacify a baby means to calm it down.
fact: In history, «pacification» means bringing peace to an area by force, often by an army, so that people stop fighting back.
fact: A euphemism is a gentle word used to hide something harsh, and «pacification» can work this way in history texts.
fact: Phrase to learn: the pacification of a region.
exercise: «The army began the pacification of the region.» What does pacification mean here? | *Bringing peace by force, so people stop fighting back | Holding a big party to celebrate | Building new roads for trade | Teaching children to read
exercise: Mum says the baby needs pacifying. What does pacifying mean? | Waking the baby up | Taking the baby outside to run | Feeding the baby a big meal | *Calming the baby down
exercise: «Soldiers arrived to begin the pacification of the valley.» Which clue tells you the harsh meaning? | The full stop at the end | Nothing, it must mean calm | *Soldiers, which suggest force | The word begin
test: Why can «pacification» be called a euphemism in history? | It is always a happy word | It means a loud noise in the street | *It sounds gentle but can hide violent actions | It is a type of pet
test: Which word is the verb related to pacification? | Passive | Pace | *Pacify | Parade
exam: Which everyday meaning of pacification is correct? | Running very fast in a race | Painting a picture of the sea | Making a loud noise in the street | *Making people calm or stopping them being angry
